---

### Technical Analysis & Concurrency Rationale

#### 1. Lehman-Yao Uncommitted Concurrent Split Invariant
```
            +---------------+
            |  Inner Node   |
            +-------+-------+
                    |
      +-------------+-------------+
      |                           |
+-----+------+   atomic-link   +--v---------+
|  Leaf Node |---------------->|  New Leaf  |
| [K1, K2]   |                 | [K3, K4]   |
+------------+                 +------------+
```
* **No Shared Locks during Traversal**: When an inner or leaf node splits, the engine creates the new right node with the upper half of the keys, links `new_right->right_sibling` to the old sibling, and atomically executes a release store on `left->right_sibling`.
* **Right-Link Invariant**: If a concurrent reader navigates to the left leaf before the parent's index pointer is updated, it observes $Key > Node.high\_key$. The reader navigates through `left->right_sibling` horizontally using lock-free atomic loads with `std::memory_order_acquire`. This guarantees that readers never block or encounter missing keys during splits.

#### 2. ABA Elimination & Memory Ordering Semantics
* **Atomic Prepend & Visibility**: New `VersionRecord` allocations are prepended to the singly linked version chain using `new_record->next.store(head, memory_order_release)` and `values[i].store(new_record, memory_order_release)`.
* **Epoch Protection against ABA**: Nodes and version records are never directly deallocated with `delete`. When an update or abort occurs, records are enqueued into `EpochManager::retire(tid, ptr)`. 
* **Ordering Barriers**: Readers load `next` and payload fields with `std::memory_order_acquire`. A memory address cannot be recycled and re-inserted until all concurrent transactions active at the time of retirement have called `EpochManager::leave()`, completely neutralizing the ABA problem.

#### 3. Serializable Snapshot Isolation (SSI) & Dynamic Anti-Dependency Safety
```
  T1 (Reader) ===== [ SIREAD Lock on Key K ]
        \
         \ rw-antidependency (T1 -> T2)
          v
  T2 (Pivot Writer) === [ Modifies Key K ] -> out_conflict = true
        \
         \ rw-antidependency (T2 -> T3)
          v
  T3 (Writer) ========= [ Overwrites Key K2 previously read by T2 ] -> in_conflict = true

  => T2 has (in_conflict && out_conflict) == true  ==> ABORT T2 (Cycle Broken)
```
* **Dangerous Structure Detection**: Serializable Snapshot Isolation anomalies (such as write skew) are characterized by serialization dependency cycles containing two consecutive $rw$-antidependency edges: $T_1 \xrightarrow{rw} T_2 \xrightarrow{rw} T_3$.
* **Deterministic Abort**: The transaction manager tracks `in_conflict` (inbound $rw$-edge) and `out_conflict` (outbound $rw$-edge) via atomic bitsets in `TxContext` and the `LockFreeSIREADLockTable`. At commit time, any transaction acting as the pivot ($in\_conflict \land out\_conflict$) is aborted, preserving strict 1-Copy Serializability (1SR) without centralized lock bottlenecks.

